    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "Hi @echasnovski! I had two issues when using mini.files and mini-git in my workflow.\r\n1. How do I open current file location in mini.files instead of opening pwd and navigating to it?\r\n2. When using MiniGit.show_at_cursor() on ``:Git diff`` commands diffs, it opens up a new window instead of navigating to the existing one. Also the new window does not have an Lspattached to it and hence I will have to manually open the file again. How do you think I can solve this issue.",
    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Hi <a class=\"user-mention notranslate\" data-hovercard-type=\"user\" data-hovercard-url=\"/users/echasnovski/hovercard\" data-octo-click=\"hovercard-link-click\" data-octo-dimensions=\"link_type:self\" href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski\">@echasnovski</a>! I had two issues when using mini.files and mini-git in my workflow.</p>\n<ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>How do I open current file location in mini.files instead of opening pwd and navigating to it?</li>\n<li>When using MiniGit.show_at_cursor() on <code class=\"notranslate\">:Git diff</code> commands diffs, it opens up a new window instead of navigating to the existing one. Also the new window does not have an Lspattached to it and hence I will have to manually open the file again. How do you think I can solve this issue.</li>\n</ol>",
    "bodyText": "Hi @echasnovski! I had two issues when using mini.files and mini-git in my workflow.\n\nHow do I open current file location in mini.files instead of opening pwd and navigating to it?\nWhen using MiniGit.show_at_cursor() on :Git diff commands diffs, it opens up a new window instead of navigating to the existing one. Also the new window does not have an Lspattached to it and hence I will have to manually open the file again. How do you think I can solve this issue.",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Ask the community for help",
        "emoji": ":pray:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>🙏</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqN",
        "isAnswerable": true,
        "name": "Q&A",
        "slug": "q-a",
        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2024-09-09T09:10:23Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> 1. How do I open current file location in mini.files instead of opening pwd and navigating to it?\r\n\r\nIt is [in documentation of the main `open()` function](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/8413efde2546be4dec9ea424bc6af346a14dffbe/doc/mini-files.txt#L694-L708).\r\n\r\n> 2. When using MiniGit.show_at_cursor() on `:Git diff` commands diffs, it opens up a new window instead of navigating to the existing one. Also the new window does not have an Lspattached to it and hence I will have to manually open the file again. How do you think I can solve this issue.\r\n\r\nOnly [showing in split is supported](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/8413efde2546be4dec9ea424bc6af346a14dffbe/doc/mini-git.txt#L281-L283). Showing in the same window is not clean enough because returning to the `:Git diff` buffer is an extra hustle as the buffer is not listed. This won't be supported.\r\n\r\nLSP server is not attached because opened buffer is not actually a file from disk, but a \"snapshot\" of it (which is reflected in the name). This [is deliberate](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1126#issuecomment-2275188925).",
            "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>How do I open current file location in mini.files instead of opening pwd and navigating to it?</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">It is <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/8413efde2546be4dec9ea424bc6af346a14dffbe/doc/mini-files.txt#L694-L708\">in documentation of the main <code class=\"notranslate\">open()</code> function</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ol start=\"2\" dir=\"auto\">\n<li>When using MiniGit.show_at_cursor() on <code class=\"notranslate\">:Git diff</code> commands diffs, it opens up a new window instead of navigating to the existing one. Also the new window does not have an Lspattached to it and hence I will have to manually open the file again. How do you think I can solve this issue.</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Only <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/8413efde2546be4dec9ea424bc6af346a14dffbe/doc/mini-git.txt#L281-L283\">showing in split is supported</a>. Showing in the same window is not clean enough because returning to the <code class=\"notranslate\">:Git diff</code> buffer is an extra hustle as the buffer is not listed. This won't be supported.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">LSP server is not attached because opened buffer is not actually a file from disk, but a \"snapshot\" of it (which is reflected in the name). This <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1126#issuecomment-2275188925\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1126/hovercard\">is deliberate</a>.</p>",
            "bodyText": "How do I open current file location in mini.files instead of opening pwd and navigating to it?\n\n\nIt is in documentation of the main open() function.\n\n\nWhen using MiniGit.show_at_cursor() on :Git diff commands diffs, it opens up a new window instead of navigating to the existing one. Also the new window does not have an Lspattached to it and hence I will have to manually open the file again. How do you think I can solve this issue.\n\n\nOnly showing in split is supported. Showing in the same window is not clean enough because returning to the :Git diff buffer is an extra hustle as the buffer is not listed. This won't be supported.\nLSP server is not attached because opened buffer is not actually a file from disk, but a \"snapshot\" of it (which is reflected in the name). This is deliberate.",
